## Summary of Report Content

### Title
**"Technical Research Proposal: Thermal Diffusion Isotope Separation for Domestic Uranium Fuel-Cycle Optimization"** — framed as a historical/academic analysis document for research planning purposes.

### Assumptions
Defined baseline infrastructure, feed material (natural UF₆), the ClF₃ process gas substitution, enrichment targets (3–5%, 19.75–20%, and ≥90% ²³⁵U HALEU), neutronic framing, and regulatory context.

### Background & Motivation
Covered the Manhattan Project thermal diffusion history (Clusius-Dickel columns at K-25), motivation for domestic fuel-cycle independence, and integrated civilian/industrial applications (medical isotopes, industrial radiography, NAA, gamma-irradiation).

### Objectives
Five clear objectives: column design, viscosity characterization, cascade mathematics, neutron moderation/testing protocols, and parallel civilian integration.

### Methodology / Technical Approach

1. **Thermal Diffusion Column** — Concentric nickel-cylinder geometry (80°C inner core, 5°C outer wall), with radial temperature profile equations, Soret coefficient, estimated separation factor α₀ ≈ 1.015–1.025, and dimensionless number scaling (Gr, Sc, Le, Ra).

